{0 Local, Remote and Live data}

Silica names three kinds of data. Each kind has its own tool, and a machine
holds all three in the data of its states.

{t
| Kind | Owner | Tool |
|------|-------|------|
| Local | the client | the data of a state |
| Remote | the server, one request for each call | {!Silica.Remote}, {!Silica.Remote.call} |
| Live | the server, sent to the client when it changes | {!Silica.Topic}, {!Silica.Source.live}, {!Silica.Live} |
}

{!Silica.Status.t} shows where Remote or Live data is.

{1:local Local data}

Local data is the data of the current state: the text of a field, an open
menu, the count of a stepper. The machine changes it with [Go]. The checker
compares and hashes snapshots, so keep functions out of the data that the
checker explores. See {!page-machines}.

{1:remote Remote data}

A Remote endpoint is declared once, in code that the server and the browser
share. It has a path, a codec for the input, a codec for the output and a codec
for the application error ([rejected]). The client sends the input with POST.
The server answers with the output, or with an application error, such as a
failed validation.

{@ocaml file=examples/shared/tutorial_api.ml[
let greeting =
  Silica.Remote.declare ~path:"/api/greeting" ~input:Silica.Codec.string
    ~output:Silica.Codec.string ~rejected:Silica.Codec.string
]}

A snapshot that needs the answer runs the call: [Remote.call endpoint input
on_answer] in the list that [during] gives for it. The call starts when the
machine enters the state. The input is a value: [during] has the snapshot, so
the input can come from its data. The answer comes back as the event that
[on_answer] makes. When the machine leaves the state first, the answer is
dropped. The key of a call is its path and its input: when a step keeps the
state but changes the input, the old call stops and a new one starts.

The answer is a {!Silica.Remote.answer}:

{t
| Constructor | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| [Ok output] | the output of the handler |
| [Rejected reason] | the handler answered [Error reason], an application error |
| [Failed error] | no output: a {!Silica.Remote.error}, [Http status], [Decode message] or [Network message] |
}

[?timeout_ms] ends a call that takes too long with
[Failed (Network "timeout")]. {!Silica.Remote.error_to_string} gives a short
sentence for an error, for a view. The {{!page-tutorial.part2}tutorial} shows a
machine with a call.

The server implements the endpoint with [Server.implement] (see
{!page-server.remote}).

{1:status Status}

{!Silica.Status.t} is a plain value for data that comes from the server:

{t
| Constructor | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| [Idle] | nothing was asked for |
| [Loading old] | an answer is on its way; [old] is the data from before, if any |
| [Ready { data; stale }] | the data; [stale] is [true] for Live data while the socket is down |
| [Failed { error; last }] | the last attempt failed; [last] is the data from before |
}

The old data stays when there is some, so a view can show it while it loads
again. {!Silica.Status.data} gives the data of any status that has it.

{@ocaml[
# Silica.Status.data (Silica.Status.Loading (Some "Hello, Ada"));;
- : string option = Some "Hello, Ada"
# Silica.Status.data
    (Silica.Status.Failed { error = Http 500; last = Some "Hello, Ada" });;
- : string option = Some "Hello, Ada"
]}

{!Silica.Remote_machine} is a ready-made machine for one endpoint. Its states
are [Idle], [Loading], [Ready], [Rejected] and [Failed]; each one keeps the
input of the last call and the data from before. The events are [Load input],
[Retry], [Reset] and the answer. This view shows each state:

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_greeting_status.mlx[
open Silica

type state = (string, string, string) Remote_machine.state
type event = (string, string, string) Remote_machine.event

let machine = Remote_machine.make ~id:"greeting-status" Tutorial_api.greeting

let view : state -> event Node.t = function
  | Idle ->
      <button on_press=(Remote_machine.Load "Ada")>"Load"</button>
  | Loading { last = None; _ } ->
      <p>"Loading."</p>
  | Loading { last = Some old; _ } ->
      <p>(Node.text (old ^ " Loading again."))</p>
  | Ready { data; _ } ->
      <div>
        <p>(Node.text data)</p>
        <button on_press=(Remote_machine.Load "Grace")>"Load again"</button>
      </div>
  | Rejected { reason; _ } ->
      <div>
        <p>(Node.text reason)</p>
        <button on_press=Remote_machine.Retry>"Try again"</button>
      </div>
  | Failed { error; last; _ } ->
      <div>
        <p>
          (Node.text (Option.value last ~default:(Remote.error_to_string error)))
        </p>
        <button on_press=Remote_machine.Retry>"Try again"</button>
      </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{1:live Live data}

Live data is owned by the server. Silica has two forms of it.

{2:topics Topics}

A topic is a named piece of server data with a state type. The server
publishes the full state under a key (see {!page-server.publish}), and each
subscriber of that key gets it with a sequence number. A snapshot subscribes
with [Source.live topic key on_update] in its [during] list. The subscription
runs while the list holds it. The event carries a {!Silica.Topic.update}: a
sequence number and a [Status.t].

- At the start, the status is [Loading None] while the socket connects.
- Each published state comes as [Ready] with [stale = false].
- When the socket goes down, the old data comes again with [stale = true],
  and the client connects again by itself.
- A fatal failure comes as [Failed].

This component of the example app follows the topic [votes]:

{@mlx file=../example/app/shared/example_votes.mlx[
open Silica
open Jsonkit.Primitives

type event = Update of int Silica_jsonkit.update | Reconnect [@@deriving json]

let event_name = function Update _ -> "update" | Reconnect -> "reconnect"

type state = Watching of int Silica_jsonkit.status [@@deriving json]

let name (Watching _) = "watching"
let states = [ "watching" ]

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"votes" ~init:(Watching Status.Idle) ~name ~states
    ~event_name
    ~during:(fun (Watching _) ->
      [
        Source.live Example_model.votes_topic "main" (fun update ->
            Update update
        );
      ]
    )
    (fun (Watching status) event ->
      match event with
      | Update { status; _ } ->
          Go (Watching status)
      | Reconnect ->
          Reenter (Watching status)
    )

let text = Node.text

let view (Watching status) : event Node.t =
  let count =
    match Status.data status with Some n -> string_of_int n | None -> "..."
  in
  <section id="votes">
    <h2>"Votes"</h2>
    <p>(text "Votes: ") <span id="votes-count">(text count)</span></p>
    ( if Status.is_stale status then
        <p id="votes-offline">"Offline, showing old data"</p>
      else
        Node.list []
    )
    ( match status with
    | Status.Failed _ ->
        <button id="votes-reconnect" on_press=Reconnect>"Reconnect"</button>
    | Status.Idle | Status.Loading _ | Status.Ready _ ->
        Node.list []
    )
  </section>

let component = Component.make machine view
let make ?key () = Component.node ?key component
]}

The names that the file takes from the rest of the example app:

- [Example_model.votes_topic] is the topic, declared in shared code as
  [let votes_topic = Silica.Topic.declare ~name:"votes" ~state:Silica.Codec.int].
- [Silica_jsonkit.update] and [Silica_jsonkit.status] are
  {!Silica.Topic.update} and {!Silica.Status.t} with the JSON functions that
  [[@@deriving json]] uses (see {!page-machines.codecs}).

[Reconnect] gives [Reenter]: the state exits and enters again, so the
subscription stops and starts again.

{2:phases Connection phases}

A state that subscribes to a topic often has phases that the view shows:
connecting, live, offline. Give them names with a dot, as sub-states of one
state, such as [watching.live] and [watching.offline]. The part before the
first dot is the state that owns the timers, calls and subscriptions, so a
step between two of its sub-states keeps them running: no [exit], no
[entry], no new [Loading None], and each pending call goes on. A name with
no dot for each phase would stop and start the subscription and every call
at each step, because each name is a state of its own.

{@ocaml[
# type phase = Connecting | Live | Offline;;
type phase = Connecting | Live | Offline
# let score = Silica.Topic.declare ~name:"score" ~state:Silica.Codec.int;;
val score : int Silica.Topic.t = <abstr>
# let phase (update : int Silica.Topic.update) =
    match update.status with
    | Ready { stale = false; _ } -> Live
    | Ready { stale = true; _ } -> Offline
    | Idle | Loading _ | Failed _ -> Connecting;;
val phase : int Silica.Topic.update -> phase = <fun>
# let machine =
    Silica.Machine.make ~id:"score" ~init:Connecting
      ~name:(function
        | Connecting -> "watching.connecting"
        | Live -> "watching.live"
        | Offline -> "watching.offline")
      ~states:[ "watching.connecting"; "watching.live"; "watching.offline" ]
      ~during:(fun _ -> [ Silica.Source.live score "main" phase ])
      (fun _ next -> Silica.Machine.Go next);;
val machine : (unit, phase, phase) Silica.Machine.t = <abstr>
# let _, actions, how = Silica.Machine.step machine Live Offline;;
val actions : phase Silica.Action.host list = []
val how : Silica.Machine.how = Silica.Machine.Kept
]}

The spec has a state and the edges for each phase, and the golden
[.views.txt] keeps up to {!Check.views_per_state} views for each of them, so
the view of each phase, such as the offline one, is in the golden file. With
one name for the whole state, the phases share those views, and a phase that
the search reaches late may not show. Keep the data of the topic and the
pending changes in the snapshot, as before.

{2:islands Islands}

A Live component runs its machine on the server, one instance for each
connection: the client sends events and gets the state. It is declared with
{!Silica.Live.declare} and placed in a view with {!Silica.Live.slot}. See
{!page-hydration.islands}.

